#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995} :activity/id #uuid "1249b882-9023-5a8e-b9f3-da0aef8069d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511492}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "ba2fdaeb-e9c0-578e-a23e-d08f48959fe8" :step/math-academy-id 35542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532715}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a0e6a5fd-e87e-5316-9084-5168c4efa1da" :step/math-academy-id 35543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173201 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a path $C$ in the $xy$-plane, the arc length $L$ of $C$ can be computed as the line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is defined parametrically as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle, \\quad a\\leq t \\leq b ,\n$$\n\nthen we can also calculate the arc length of $C$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b}||\\mathbf r'(t)||\\,\\textrm d t = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ we say that the **arc-length element** $\\textrm ds$ for a parametrically defined curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, we can think of $\\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t.$ By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C.$ We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "56e8bdd3-5303-5eeb-82d3-ef837773c38f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65361afd-5d8b-59a0-8bed-041764bc0685" :step/math-academy-id 35544 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173202 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that a curve $C$ is defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nWe've already seen that a polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula for the arc length of a polar curve, the arc length of $C$ can be calculated as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as the line integral\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ the arc-length element $\\textrm ds$ for a curve can also be defined using polar coordinates as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll build up a geometric understanding of this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's use this result to calculate some line integrals across polar curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "4850750a-5b8d-5c03-88c1-a2b832a313ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5801 :tutorial/title "The Arc Length Element for Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "665cda4d-3863-540c-afb3-a1cde7ebd6d9" :step/math-academy-id 35565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173203 :tutorial/content "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y,$ respectively, corresponding to the change in $t.$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t.\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}](Δt)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s\n\n\\approx\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65578a76-05f7-551a-acbd-956ae6831965" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5802 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "46feb8c2-5068-5992-a3a2-858b37d8c39e" :step/math-academy-id 35569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173204 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve $r= r(\\theta)$ defined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r,$ and\n\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'.$ The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ≈(Δr)^{2}+(rΔ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =Δ{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =[{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}](Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b310dc84-9da7-5492-9639-145086fe7403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5803 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6cd1a226-a48b-5c1c-9eed-9c80bd2de431" :step/math-academy-id 35570}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172176}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483441} :activity/id #uuid "495233f9-f743-506d-b5fc-eaec14701f70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b071591-3de0-5c1f-8aeb-668f34070824" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c4079-2e4b-58ce-bcdd-cef5dccc4c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd78b7-9d7a-58b9-8cb4-d7bec505415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482890} {:answer/id #uuid "33feb924-d253-5720-9471-b530f5057780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482903} {:answer/id #uuid "4170ee29-2690-59a0-ac02-7ef811bc4abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482922} {:answer/id #uuid "42a9d64f-13f1-500c-89f4-8b8e1616488f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b124e2-32da-5651-8541-39b8419c32ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22b679e5-bd2f-5f85-b6e8-97dc0feb70a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149799" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+\\theta )^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }({\\theta }^{2}+1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c48a8d6-bb4a-559a-912c-74a8d59f12d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614ec109-ddf5-5d69-8b6f-788cffd7d795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} {:answer/id #uuid "654af1d0-1b91-562a-9226-854b3fe81f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482990} {:answer/id #uuid "6bec234f-7e3b-515c-a2ad-33a2c8025357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482999} {:answer/id #uuid "960e21ae-a0b1-56a4-a8e3-7aa555730f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483072} {:answer/id #uuid "c60010fa-7943-5d78-8597-6d6fec062ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} :answer-field/id #uuid "87114391-cb4d-5092-83cc-b2e18baed326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dcd1fd46-503a-5471-9362-496d88e411cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-149819" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{6}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})[{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "73aaaa88-a71a-530e-8d01-e165dd7e3bfc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500ad374-633d-5acc-8fc6-2c954b7ceee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482954} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf2602-5ddf-5b93-9c8f-85bf86b1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} {:answer/id #uuid "c4195c77-54de-55db-b8bc-7aa9584420e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483138} {:answer/id #uuid "dd638516-5b00-5d2e-b7d7-70996ad3e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483185} {:answer/id #uuid "eb789a46-0304-547b-8796-b1b162722c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} :answer-field/id #uuid "416c49b7-a3b7-5531-9507-24f1c4541b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6017b841-30bb-5249-81e7-cac41367e88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123548" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "748e2a73-740f-5666-bb51-de197b0e3f99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f880bd39-bc57-5d41-b262-cb0554da3861" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483454}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482801}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511645} :activity/id #uuid "2e6736bd-345f-5eb1-b1d1-69bf9d392abd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0accf-355f-56fb-b473-52acb69bd23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257002} {:answer/id #uuid "1a282cf9-e726-5893-9709-a1869d24dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{17+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257005} {:answer/id #uuid "58bdd781-32a3-5cea-bf8a-eda0cdb4360a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{12+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +10{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257551} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2de95a-65cf-5b52-864b-9114a600f1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{12+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257887} {:answer/id #uuid "d1432cc5-bba9-5179-80ef-98471b08f92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adf0afe-c0b4-5de7-8baf-d650968a622b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77c73302-f62f-524d-b1a1-cbae03250f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-123395" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc83cbf45ec3025ad33552ce889c7ecb2daf73b426bc2fd10ec932f3311c92a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{17+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd7e7ed-0cb5-5f2a-9906-20fa4a290dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256894} {:answer/id #uuid "22a84c4e-ebcd-5545-8a90-d6cd63c6a644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257070} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6fff17-f37b-5bca-8413-fcf9937009c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257313} {:answer/id #uuid "785374b9-120b-5f1f-a6e2-69129997ff6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257847} {:answer/id #uuid "b514f795-53a7-5471-a8c9-56185b112376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256894} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe556346-c85f-5997-afb5-d4236fb40204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4d5a329-2172-581a-b274-24fd141bca53" :question/math-academy-id "q-123371" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b6490495c297b49d394b0f7c494fb35b710c8c4520ab898783877abdd079013.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{20-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510875} {:answer/id #uuid "5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510978} {:answer/id #uuid "8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} {:answer/id #uuid "c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{\\theta }}{\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511197} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\theta }}{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149800" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta }$ for $1\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01eaff75-9bd9-584b-8dac-798d298e7fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510777} {:answer/id #uuid "0d033828-6617-5a30-b0e6-4a3c14d14576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510806} {:answer/id #uuid "19428a95-6f1a-515e-aa64-ad5b042f4aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510840} {:answer/id #uuid "51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510956} {:answer/id #uuid "e233503b-2829-57e2-afff-b160c60d6d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510956} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bc4d54-53d7-5960-8596-76e1189178c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" :question/math-academy-id "q-123634" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} {:answer/id #uuid "3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510889} {:answer/id #uuid "5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510970} {:answer/id #uuid "bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511174} {:answer/id #uuid "dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :question/math-academy-id "q-123643" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511657}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510756}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532756} :activity/id #uuid "822bcaa0-c52b-52f8-b781-cc9986a54dc7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17652adb-f0bd-5e06-b55e-fb60b41d8171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447094} {:answer/id #uuid "392cdecf-382d-5eaf-8512-13e93f090b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} {:answer/id #uuid "bb292c1a-79be-51a1-91b7-2783d7d709cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447133} {:answer/id #uuid "e84048f5-bc2b-5cb4-a79e-ebf71a21e3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447139} {:answer/id #uuid "fdef13fa-c9e2-5073-8dc0-04da2e76ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cca78f2-2d1d-5264-9180-c7409ad32bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "586f6a61-ad62-56e5-9a2c-611ea0a2819c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123379" :question/problem "![](images/19/19725cfd8068973c899fcb4f54abcebbbfcd24bd68aec510b9d41cee2c2cd02a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{20-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"} :step/id #uuid "cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532063} {:answer/id #uuid "61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532151} {:answer/id #uuid "91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532265} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532339} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-123558" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77"}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531934}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543661} :activity/id #uuid "1f3a6185-41af-5ad8-935e-01846db70555" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f17b3f-b619-5663-a742-8cd16caad422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363373} {:answer/id #uuid "2f268941-c5e1-5f87-9aff-31416118fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363374} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf36ad-cc0f-5781-9b02-c40c452e845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+12{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363375} {:answer/id #uuid "eac417bd-f5e6-53b0-85c1-fea6d7f50ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+40{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} :answer-field/id #uuid "388c2572-dc24-57d0-9cb1-bc6bf8263984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd418bfc-f7c5-506e-bc72-5623879aa6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149798" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta +60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4+60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4bc5a2d-3e93-51e5-9f99-7400e1837432"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ae34bece-dd6d-55aa-a39e-6fffe351a9e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0081acc5-b8a0-5432-9f86-16b58fe1f445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +10\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447091} {:answer/id #uuid "1015c73e-191b-5d23-bc1d-08512f03b481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+6{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447093} {:answer/id #uuid "369565c3-561a-50ca-9be4-d934f4abc68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447100} {:answer/id #uuid "609847c3-196c-5c58-9386-82fb40230109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +8{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447111} {:answer/id #uuid "8a907a7b-6b1a-50eb-9d99-ac2cfde55650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447111} :answer-field/id #uuid "970ad32f-3bc8-5eae-a83f-06f38e693fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c80223d-79b2-5d97-8d88-3fa6275a012a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123377" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b8ef36c29d1babc5c3ccf1d474678a18ed5937b883e026d18d5c7ea1aaaa3f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +1+8{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0a58790-b2fc-5728-aa3b-02bd91182c99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac3d5894-f4c5-5d9e-ab7c-cd4cec7b9228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405031} {:answer/id #uuid "098ef428-487b-52de-8bb3-157513d5af5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405032} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec24e38-78a7-59a2-bcbd-116c50a31d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405033} {:answer/id #uuid "2fff1e42-09de-569f-8b26-6742481f41f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405034} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7a56e-be07-55b4-a841-3621b9f27de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405034} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aaf3974-45a7-5d36-b332-ebb301287f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123458" :question/problem "Which of the following gives ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s$ with the arc-length integrand simplified pointwise in the given polar angle $\\theta$ (on each smooth piece, without reparametrizing), where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhere $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta>0$, $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nApplying the arc-length formula piecewise between the finitely many zeros of $r$, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea17984d-b1fd-5109-a5c3-c2c1166176dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec25039-c0fd-56ca-b984-bd9349c79ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363365} {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd3e2e0-b449-5ae9-bfe5-39ae00b8ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363367} {:answer/id #uuid "827145a1-4259-51ff-8504-aef48c2e2ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363368} {:answer/id #uuid "0a472974-80e2-5c15-a84b-22f9c2f5a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5703c778-a247-5102-af88-f5464e7312d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "929c6f43-55a6-50e3-98c8-0627bab8c6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-149788" :question/problem "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{-\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-{e}^{-\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{-\\theta })}^{2}+{(-{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{-2\\theta }+{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f760da62-3000-5957-aaa0-e2654d0801a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543651}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542615}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553606} :activity/id #uuid "380fb825-5be1-580a-990c-bf11f8d48700" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} {:answer/id #uuid "0560e91f-841f-5133-b19f-414819f58310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2{e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362985} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c2c04d-6401-5b56-9894-aeead7dbe986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362986} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc5fcaa-258a-5aeb-90ce-1bd2af6582b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362987} {:answer/id #uuid "a210ea76-975d-59a6-8399-df6d3b035510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b62b53e-864d-5c41-bf41-a59840c727e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2b06f7c-408a-5dfe-b281-0fde1f80e146" :question/math-academy-id "q-123354" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33f9b851125443ff738fa4c3aedcbeaf8a202d05caa06747e74262c0ae89bd9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20042ad6-2b50-5151-ad49-bb4eef91cd09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336fa244-06f6-591f-8966-fdfd73c6a423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +2)}^{1/2}-8}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257235} {:answer/id #uuid "58895e89-7919-506e-994d-1c2dbe196d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257550} {:answer/id #uuid "c128e791-18c2-5301-8880-f7b4f01bfcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258470} {:answer/id #uuid "e274d3fe-784b-5699-8844-849e5934ed3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +2)}^{3/2}-8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258753} {:answer/id #uuid "f808119a-e030-585c-82c2-c558a63e63a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +4)}^{1/2}-8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257550} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4736e82-40af-567d-b32a-974a616d81a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5451ce0-d618-51ea-8499-28c757a17f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-123587" :question/problem "![](images/99/994b8cd2134d6d1e9a1e89559e462128c05fd5d3e7f4755c51931f3cf7f37268.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={\\theta }^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({\\theta }^{2})}^{2}+{(2\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+4{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}(4+{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\theta \\sqrt{4+{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\theta \\sqrt{4+{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{{(4+{\\theta }^{2})}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22e62c73-21df-5fda-9d79-ba9cdeb1a7a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04289a9d-17cf-573a-9701-d41c64ef2496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400109} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd6606e-b516-5f6f-9f4b-c6f100ea81db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400110} {:answer/id #uuid "41205926-9dd0-53e7-b1aa-ade02572e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400111} {:answer/id #uuid "be2d7cad-36f0-55ab-b08f-a68a53ffb570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} {:answer/id #uuid "4933aa14-2d33-5b3a-973b-f6175f55c3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bff7a0b-d96e-5598-95de-cbbd3ac2a012" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffdfd696-312e-58bb-aa35-858898cb0c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-123519" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "76362486-adbf-5459-b248-d212c1251f2c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc039c6-5665-5166-aa53-9a15c6c4c91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} {:answer/id #uuid "8b464717-8539-5469-8060-dac626a0833e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273588} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ea6766-0861-5a8d-8643-ea7eabf5bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274253} {:answer/id #uuid "d920ab5e-c0b5-5fd4-a193-1324a5a2e7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274284} {:answer/id #uuid "e41b2b91-8816-5099-b2fa-6f471a4a0905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} :answer-field/id #uuid "49db0e6a-b203-52c9-99a3-d2fa536b85d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4554a25e-9962-5d01-9655-91afa5f0b772" :question/math-academy-id "q-149815" :question/problem "![](images/62/624bb949d80c1da50cba951b00210a2a26aeb3c44d77d4c2eed69c8e3ba0f4d4.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d0d777b-efed-57ba-b08b-6f64a58115f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2aeb4a-6148-5344-8241-a30ca0414bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+5{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447092} {:answer/id #uuid "369bbef4-e31e-5ef6-9afb-de7d3e21d28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447101} {:answer/id #uuid "39da32fb-4fd3-56f4-8b48-27bfa06dac2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+8{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447105} {:answer/id #uuid "7d101eaf-b053-5e48-a386-fa7d17ca7d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{5+4{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447114} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9bc000-67c4-53d2-b325-fa53d6ea89eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+7{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447101} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dcb75d-dac3-5c81-8f6e-1e4763779208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb00dc0e-66fe-5382-b23d-00ac1fa661b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123559" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\pi /3\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({csc}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-3{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "952b8495-fb82-537a-8ec9-22ad3d4e7fad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553607}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552854}]}
